WHAT: make the app a wk node -- default to the `wk` QPA platform and statically
      import the three plugins wasm cannot dlopen.
WHY:  two things a wasm32-wasip2 Qt node needs that a desktop build gets free:
        * QT_QPA_PLATFORM. plugins/qt's qtbase was configured
          QT_QPA_DEFAULT_PLATFORM=offscreen (it predates the wk QPA plugin), so
          an unmodified app renders to nowhere and never opens a surface. Set
          only when the environment does not already say, so
          QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen stays available for bisecting failures.
        * Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN. No dlopen on wasm. Without QWkIntegrationPlugin,
          QApplication aborts with "no Qt platform plugin could be initialized"
          and an EMPTY plugin list -- it reads like a broken Qt, but it is an
          unreferenced symbol. QSvgPlugin/QSvgIconPlugin matter more here than
          in the other Qt ports: qalculateqtsettings.h:31 defines
          LOAD_APP_ICON(x) as QIcon(":/icons/apps/scalable/" x ".svg") and
          icons.qrc is 53 SVGs, so without them the app has no icons at all.
      The component-type link objects and the font resource are handled by the
      port's own CMakeLists (upstream has none), not here.
      Both hunks are guarded by #ifdef __wasi__, so a desktop build of this
      tree is byte-for-byte unaffected.
UPSTREAM: as-is in spirit -- this is the standard static-Qt deployment shape.
      It is wk-specific only in naming OUR platform plugin.
--- a/src/main.cpp
+++ b/src/main.cpp
@@ -34,6 +34,22 @@
 #include "qalculatewindow.h"
 #include "qalculateqtsettings.h"
 
+// --- wk node ---------------------------------------------------------------
+// wasm has no dlopen, so Qt cannot discover a plugin at runtime: every plugin
+// is linked in as an archive and named here. Without the QWkIntegrationPlugin
+// line QApplication aborts with "no Qt platform plugin could be initialized"
+// and an EMPTY available-plugins list, which reads like a broken Qt build but
+// is a missing symbol reference. The two Svg plugins are load-bearing for this
+// app in particular: icons.qrc is 53 SVGs and qalculateqtsettings.h's
+// LOAD_APP_ICON/LOAD_COLORED_ICON only ever name ".svg", so without them every
+// toolbar button, menu action and the window icon are null QIcons.
+#ifdef __wasi__
+#include <QtPlugin>
+Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN(QWkIntegrationPlugin)
+Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN(QSvgPlugin)
+Q_IMPORT_PLUGIN(QSvgIconPlugin)
+#endif
+
 #ifdef _WIN32
 #	include <windows.h>
 #endif
@@ -46,6 +62,18 @@ QTranslator translator, translator_qt, translator_qtbase;
 
 int main(int argc, char **argv) {
 
+#ifdef __wasi__
+	// plugins/qt/sysroot's qtbase was configured with
+	// QT_QPA_DEFAULT_PLATFORM=offscreen -- it predates the wk plugin -- so
+	// without this the app comes up on a platform that renders to nowhere and
+	// never opens a wk surface. An explicit QT_QPA_PLATFORM in the node's
+	// environment still wins, which is what makes `QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen`
+	// usable for separating "Qt is broken" from "the wk QPA is broken".
+	if(!qEnvironmentVariableIsSet("QT_QPA_PLATFORM")) {
+		qputenv("QT_QPA_PLATFORM", "wk");
+	}
+#endif
+
 #ifdef _WIN32
 	if(!qEnvironmentVariableIsSet("QT_QPA_PLATFORM")) {
 		qputenv("QT_QPA_PLATFORM", "windows:altgr");
